@* TRAV-1 (controller decisions 3+4): the ONE arrow-traversal component both
   the Narrative block (EventDateAndPlacesSection) and the new Chronology
   block (EventChronologySection, PopoverSectionProviders.cs) render --
   "you can traverse them in essentially the same way," the owner's own
   words, realized as literally the same component rather than two
   near-identical renderings. Extracted from EventDateAndPlacesSection's own
   former `RenderNavArrow` static helper (a RenderTreeBuilder-only method,
   with no state of its own that could survive across renders) into a real
   component for exactly one reason: decision 4's dwell-hover verse peek
   needs a timer that OUTLIVES a single render (started on pointer-enter,
   fired ~350-400ms later unless cancelled first) -- a RenderFragment
   closure is rebuilt fresh every render (see EventDateAndPlacesSection's
   own "Fresh every render... a dictionary declared outside this closure
   would keep accumulating" doc comment for why that discipline exists),
   but a genuine Blazor component instance persists across them, which is
   what a live CancellationTokenSource needs. Both callers open this via
   `builder.OpenComponent<ArrowNav>(...)` from their own RenderTreeBuilder
   bodies -- EventTimelineDirectionSection (now retired) already
   established that exact "open a real component from imperative
   RenderTreeBuilder code" pattern in this same file, for PassageList.

   DWELL GRAMMAR (decision 4, "quick pass-over -> nothing; dwell -> a small
   hover box with the target event's verse text; click still traverses"):
   pointer-enter starts a fresh timer (DwellTiming.PeekDelayMs, the ONE
   constant CHAP-HOVER-1's own chapter-head peek also uses -- see that
   file's own doc comment); pointer-leave before it fires cancels outright
   (no peek ever shown -- "not super sensitive, so some delay so that
   you're not accidentally getting hover boxes all the time," the owner's
   own words). CLICK is wired completely independently (`Commit`, below) --
   it never reads or waits on dwell state at all, so a genuine click always
   commits immediately regardless of whether the peek happened to be
   mid-dwell, already showing, or never touched at all.

   PEEK-TRUNC-1 (owner defect report, 2026-08-24, verbatim: "menus appearing
   on hover from arrow hover are getting cut off. needs to be truncated to
   an expandable menu limit one verse."): the peek's own CONTENT, DISMISS
   grammar, and PLACEMENT all change here; the OPEN side immediately above
   is untouched.
   - CONTENT: the target event's FULL title (Adjacent.Label -- TITLE-WRAP-1's
     own "the complete name is one dwell away, always") as a header, plus
     exactly ONE resolved verse by default, plus the house quiet reveal
     affordance (RevealControls -- "more (n) . all (N) . less") once more
     than one verse resolved. Used DIRECTLY here rather than through
     PassageList/PassageBlockBuilder: that component's own two truncation
     axes count/cut at the wrong granularity for this box -- Cap counts
     whole PASSAGE ENTRIES (a single contiguous block can itself hold many
     verses, so capping at one ENTRY is not "exactly one VERSE"), and
     ClampVerses is a STATIC per-entry clamp whose only further reveal is
     the WHOLE CHAPTER (MiniReaderExpand), never a graduated one-more-verse
     step. RevealControls itself -- already its own generic, standalone
     component (Total/Default/Shown/Step, nothing about WHAT is being
     shown) -- IS the shared piece the owner's own spec asks to reuse, so
     it is used directly, verse-by-verse, rather than contorting a caller
     whose own grouping doesn't fit. Nothing is duplicated: RevealControls
     is still the ONE implementation of "more/all/less," just with a
     second direct consumer alongside PassageList's own internal one.
   - DISMISS: pointer-leave used to dismiss the peek immediately, no grace
     period, because a non-interactive box never needed the pointer to
     travel INTO it. Now that `more`/`less` live INSIDE the box, the
     pointer must cross the small visual gap between the arrow/role-caption
     stack and the peek (margin .4rem, app.css) to reach them. The SAME
     @onpointerenter/@onpointerleave pair on this component's own wrapping
     element (unchanged below) already covers that whole subtree -- the
     peek box is a DOM DESCENDANT of the wrapper, not a sibling, see the
     markup below -- but the browser still fires a transient leave+enter
     PAIR on the wrapper mid-transit (real pixels in that gap that the
     wrapper's own un-padded box doesn't cover, so hit-testing there
     resolves to something outside the wrapper's subtree for an instant).
     ScheduleDismiss/CancelDismiss below give that transit a short GRACE
