     elapses), the peek still dismisses on its own -- "pointer-leave (of
     arrow+box combined) dismisses," the owner's own spec, just not
     necessarily on the exact tick a true departure is first detected.
     Mid-dwell pointer-leave (the peek not shown YET) is UNCHANGED: still
     cancels outright, no grace -- PEEK-1's own "quick pass-over -> nothing"
     law never needed a grace period and still doesn't; a grace period only
     ever makes sense for something that's already showing and might
     legitimately be travelled INTO.
   - PLACEMENT: never clipped by its own real clipping boundary -- the
     enclosing `.popover` (F1 fix round below has the full "why not the
     viewport" story). OnAfterRenderAsync below measures this component's
     own wrapper, once per peek OPEN (never re-measured while it stays
     open -- the SAME "measure once, on open" discipline ExplorerPopover's
     own verse/pane anchoring already established, see that file's own
     header comment), via reader.js's new getElementRect -- an
     ElementReference-keyed sibling of that file's own selector-keyed
     getVerseAnchorRect. A selector would only ever resolve
     document.querySelector's own FIRST match: up to FOUR ArrowNav
     instances can be mounted at once (prior x following, narrative x
     chronology), so a shared CSS-class selector would silently measure
     the wrong instance's anchor for every side but one -- the same "pass
     the real element, not a selector" reasoning reader.js's own
     capturePointer already established for SplitDivider's analogous
     multi-instance concern. Whichever side (above/below the wrapper) has
     more real room wins, and that side's own max-height is capped to
     exactly the room available -- "never clipped" without needing to
     predict content height up front: genuine overflow (an expanded,
     many-verse peek) degrades to app.css's own `overflow-y: auto`, an
     internal scroll, never an off-screen spill. A quick pass-over that
     never dwells long enough to show a peek never measures anything (no
     JS interop call) -- matching this component's own pre-existing
     "never a fetch until the dwell actually fires" discipline one layer
     further out.

   FIX ROUND 1 (controller review, PASS/PASS, one Important + two Minors):
   - F1 (Important, reviewer live-repro): the original placement math
     budgeted against window.innerHeight. The peek's own real clipping
     boundary is the enclosing `.popover` (position:fixed, its own
     max-height:calc(100vh - 4rem), routinely SHORTER than the viewport,
     clips every descendant including a position:absolute one like this
     peek) -- reader.js's own getElementRect now measures THAT ancestor
     directly (PopoverTop/PopoverBottom, ElementRectDto below) and
     OnAfterRenderAsync's own flip/max-height math budgets against it,
     never the viewport. The owner's own original defect ("cut off...
     needs to be truncated") could otherwise resurface once expanded --
     confirmed live by the reviewer: the default one-verse peek fit fine,
     but clicking `all` on a many-verse peek spilled ~269px past the
     popover's own bottom edge, its internal overflow-y:auto scrollbar
     never engaging because its budget had come from the wrong frame.
     PEEK-3b (tests/ux/event-timeline.spec.ts) pins this permanently.
   - F2 (Minor, reviewer finding): the server's own 20-verse-per-chapter
     cap (HOTFIX-4's GroupCount) is disclosed again -- ComputeTruncatedBy
     below computes it directly from _peekVerses' own already-flowing
     GroupCount field (VerseTextResolver.ResolveGroupsAsync always
     attaches it; nothing new to fetch), rendered as a small, honest,
     non-interactive `.popover-arrow-peek-truncated` note whenever it's
     nonzero -- RevealControls' own Total/"all (N)" stays honest about
     what THIS peek actually holds (never inflated to a count it can't
     really reveal); the separate note is what tells the truth about
     verses the wire never sent at all.
   - F3 (Minor): Dispose() now also calls HidePeekState(), matching
     Commit()'s own symmetry -- every disposal path leaves this instance's
